# Research Freshness

- Before adding or upgrading dependencies, check current crate/package docs, latest stable version, maintenance state, security advisories, and relevant standards.
- For PQC and ZK, prefer primary sources: NIST FIPS 203 (ML-KEM), FIPS 204 (ML-DSA), FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA), CFRG drafts, IETF PQC-in-TLS drafts, arkworks-rs upstream, CIRCL upstream, audit reports, and peer-reviewed papers.
- Track NIST PQC milestones: HQC selected March 2025 as backup KEM; CISA PQC procurement advisory January 2026; CNSA 2.0 deadline January 2027 for new National Security Systems acquisitions. Reflect these dates in `docs/progress.md` deadlines.
- Pin what the project uses in lockfiles, but cite the source and date for why the choice was made in nearby docs or research notes.
- Do not use stale model knowledge for security-sensitive APIs. Use web/repo research when choosing crates, protocol parameters, mobile platform APIs, or ZK toolchains.
- Maintain research notes under `docs/research/` when a decision depends on papers, standards, benchmark data, or market/security claims.
- Separate facts from hypotheses. Mark open research questions explicitly instead of coding around assumptions.
- Before major decisions, search wisely for best current practice: compare at least two viable options, check official docs and security/advisory status, record why the selected option is best for this phase.
- Stop and ask if current research contradicts project rules, makes a selected dependency look unsafe, or reveals a better approach with major architecture impact.
